    ---
    
    # THE SHAPING OF THE BERG
    
    ## Being the Compiled Recension of the Three Tellings, Set Down in the Archive-Ice at Light Ingjeizhi During the Sixty-Third Clear Sky of the Great Thaw
    
    *Assembled from Aurora liturgical chant (Lens-Cathedral cycle), Nightshelf smoke-oath transcription (fire-keeper lineage of Chill-breaker Sanctervilevia~ Ice), and deep-water pressure-song as rendered by the Wake-Singer pod of Kzul-gnark-gnash into vibration-markings on whale-bone tablet. The compiler has preserved the contradictions. Where the traditions disagree, both stand. Where they agree, attend carefully — for agreement among three inheritors of one corpse is rarer than open water.*
    
    ---
    
    ## I. THE TIME BEFORE NAMING
    
    Before the Berg had been named, before ice had been separated from fire, before above had been distinguished from below—
    
    Before the aurora had color. Before darkness required an opposite. Before bone knew it was not stone, before blood knew it was not sea—
    
    There was the body.
    
    The orca say: it swam. Vast beyond measure, older than hunger, moving through a medium that was not water because water had not yet been separated from anything else. It had jaws. It had a crown of light that was not yet light. It had a heart whose rhythm was the only rhythm, and therefore was not yet rhythm, because there was nothing to beat against. It was kin. It breathed, and its breath was the medium it swam through, undifferentiated, the exhale indistinguishable from the inhale.
    
    The Aurora texts say otherwise. There was no swimming, no breath, no jaws. There was a radiance — a body of pure potential suspended in generative darkness, luminous and still. The crown preceded the body. The light preceded the form. What the orca call a living creature, the Aurora tradition names a *divine field*: a substrate without edges, crystal stretching to the frozen horizon in every direction, of which the Berg is only a fragment. It did not move because there was nowhere to move. It did not breathe because it *was* the air.
    
    The Nightshelf fire-keepers record a third account, older than either, preserved in smoke-oath and tunnel-chant: there was a body, yes. It was warm. Its veins ran with something that was not yet fire but would become fire when the world required burning. Its organs held a pressure that was not yet force but would become force when flesh was carved into geography. It was dark inside the body. It was always dark inside the body.
    
    All three traditions agree on one detail, and only one:
    
    Before the shapers, the Obelisk waited.
    
    ---
    
    ## II. THE THING THAT WAS ALREADY THERE
    
    It is embedded in the earliest pressure-songs. It appears in the Aurora liturgy's sixty-first verse, though no commentary explains its inclusion. The fire-keepers do not name it aloud — they mark it as a gap in the smoke-oath, a place where the chant skips a beat and the brazier-flame dips blue.
    
    The Omega Obelisk. M7-6HH39N, in the old kill-tally notation that no living Wake-Singer fully reads.
    
    Seven feet of whale-bone darkened past recognition, wound at the base with coral that might be decoration or scarification or neither. Kill-counts carved in a script that predates the god. Sigils in no language the shapers used, in no language the orca sang, in no language the aurora illuminated.
    
    The orca say: when the god was young, the Obelisk was already old. It was found inside the body — or the body grew around it, the way flesh closes over a splinter, incorporating what it cannot expel. A wound the god was born with. A foreign object in divine anatomy.
    
    The Aurora texts mention it once, in the negative: *the light did not touch it. The light would not touch it. The light, which touches all things that exist within the made world, passed around it as water passes around a stone that belongs to a different river.*
    
    The fire-keepers say only this: the ice weeps near it. The deep ice, which remembers everything, remembers nothing about the Obelisk. It is a gap in the memory. A hole in the shape of a pillar. Something from a world before this world, carried over or left behind, and the god who became the Berg could no more read its markings than we can.
    
    Near its apex, three symbols were deliberately gouged out — defaced so savagely the bone shows white beneath. No tradition claims this act. No tradition explains it. The compiler notes only that the defacement appears to predate the shaping of the Berg itself.
    
    Before the shapers arrived, the Obelisk waited. After the shaping, it remained. It is the one fact all three traditions share and none can explain.
    
    ---
    
    ## III. THE KILLING, THE GIFT, THE BURROWING
    
    Here the traditions diverge beyond reconciliation.
    
    ### *The Wake-Singer Account, as Given in Pressure-Song:*
    
    Something killed the god.
    
    The Wake-Singers do not say what. Their oldest songs describe the blow but not the striker: a force that struck the body at the place where the jaws met the skull, splitting the divine flesh along a seam that had not existed before impact. The blow opened the body like a carcass. The frozen sea — which is not ocean but absence, the void where no divine body fell — flooded in through the wound. The god's blood thickened and went solid. The god's bones locked into the angles of ridgelines. The god's breath caught in its throat and never finished leaving.
    
    The Glow-Fissure is where the killing blow landed.
    
    That is why its light matches no known source. That is why it pulses — not as light pulses, but as a wound pulses, the last arterial rhythm of something that has not finished dying. It is not an emanation. It is a hemorrhage.
    
    And the proof — the Wake-Singers hold this above all other evidence — is the Dreaming Tooth entrusted now to Grandmother Deep, who took it from the current when the ocean floor buckled and the shamans looked away. A piece of the god removed by the first orca shaman, Rrak-gruk-krul the Jaw-Breaker, who dove into the open wound when the blood was still cooling and pried the tooth from the divine mandible with nothing but force and grief. Proof the god had jaws. Proof the god was flesh. Proof the god was kin.
    
    The Tooth is larger than any living orca. It hums with a frequency that preceded sound — a vibration that makes teeth ache because it remembers being teeth. It still dreams because the god does. It is not an artifact. It is an amputation.
    
    The first shaman held it and wept. The second held it and dove into the deep and did not return. The third held it and began the blood-rituals that persist to this day — not worship but medicine, the desperate ministrations of a pod trying to resuscitate one of their own.
    
    Three inheritors; one corpse; no will.
    
    ### *The Aurora Liturgical Account:*
    
    The god was not killed. The god gave.
    
    In the sixty-second verse of the Lens-Cathedral cycle, the All-Radiant Body — unnamed, because naming would diminish it — ceased its luminescence at the crown and directed all remaining light downward, into its own substance, crystallizing itself from the top of the skull toward the extremities. This was not death but *transformation*: the divine choosing to become habitable, the field choosing to become a place, the radiance choosing to become architecture.
    
    The first light struck at what is now the Aurora Stack. The brow of the god. The crown, where thought had pooled brightest. And where the light struck, it did not merely freeze — it *refracted*. The spires of the Stack are not carved ice but crystallized thought, the god's last intention made structural, made navigable, made beautiful in the way that sacrifice is beautiful when it succeeds.
    
    From the crown, the light moved downward. From the thought, the substance:
    
    From the brow-ridges, the crystal galleries where the aurora pools and speaks.
    From the vertebrae, the ridgeline passages that connect surface to depth.
    From the open eye, the northern sea — frozen, watching, never fully shut.
    From the teeth, the ridgeline stones that ring the eastern face.
    From the breath, the trade winds that carry krill-scent to the shores.
    From the sinew, the bridges between spire and shelf.
    From the marrow, the deep ice that remembers everything the body knew.
    
    The shapers were not separate from the god. They *were* the light — the final emanation, directed inward, carving flesh into form. And when the crystallization was complete, the shapers did not leave. They became the aurora itself: the perpetual crown of light that dances over the Berg, still shaping, still measuring, still refracting the god's last thought into a language the living can almost read.
    
    *The compiler notes: the Aurora tradition does not mention the Glow-Fissure. This absence has been remarked upon by every commentator since Hainou mewei'Frostglass, and no liturgist has offered explanation. What the Aurora does not speak of is sometimes more instructive than what it proclaims.*
    
    ### *The Nightshelf Fire-Keeper Account:*
    
    The body did not swim and the body did not shine. The body lay in darkness, as all bodies do, and it was warm.
    
    The shapers came not from outside but from within. They were born in the body's warmth — in the volcanic pressure of the god's interior, where the blood moved too slowly to cool and the organs remembered their function even as the form around them solidified. They did not carve from the surface downward, as the Aurora claims. They burrowed from the inside out.
    
    From the god's heart, still beating: the fire-cores, each one a clot of divine blood too dense to freeze, still pulsing with the old rhythm.
    From the god's veins: the tunnels, following paths the blood had carved long before any shaper put flipper to stone.
    From the god's liver: the forge-chambers where mineral concentrated into workable metal.
    From the god's belly: the deep aquifers that taste of salt and iron.
    From the god's lungs: the ventilation shafts, still cycling air the body no longer needs but cannot stop producing.
    
    The Nightfall Shelf is a living organ. It has forgotten its function but not its warmth. The fire-keepers are not miners — they are custodians. Chill-breaker Sanctervilevia~ Ice, the first of the lineage, is recorded in the smoke-oaths as she-who-entered-the-still-beating-chamber, who laid her flipper against the wall of the god's heart and felt it pulse, once, under her touch, and understood: the god is not dead. The god is sleeping. The body remembers what the name forgot.
    
    And so the first fire-keepers sealed the chambers from the inside. They barricaded the veins. They locked the doors — not against intruders from without, but against the body's own spasms: the god stirring in its sleep, its immune system flaring at breach-points, the flesh remembering it was once alive and refusing to be only stone.
    
    The Glow-Fissure is the god's last exhalation, still cycling. The corruption that bleeds upward from the breach-points is not infection — it is the body trying to heal. The cold pulse that shortens and quickens is not decay but respiration, slow as geology, patient as the god itself.
    
    What the shapers sealed, they sealed for reason. What was locked inside the god's body was locked from the inside, by those who understood the body best.
    
    The deep-singers say otherwise? Let them. They never touched the heart.
    
    ---
    
    ## IV. THE DIVISION
    
    The world split because the shapers could not agree on what they had made.
    
    Those who claimed the crown said: the god gave itself willingly; the Berg is a temple; the work is finished; the light is the inheritance.
    
    Those who claimed the heart said: the god still breathes; the Berg is a ward; the work is custodianship; the warmth is the inheritance.
    
    Those who circled the body in the frozen sea said: the god was murdered; the Berg is a corpse; there is no inheritance because you built your homes on our mother's bones.
    
    The Stack rose into the light and called it faith. The Shelf sank into the dark and called it duty. The pods circled the shore and called it grief.
    
    Three inheritors. One corpse. No will.
    
    The Flipper Accord, signed in this present age of plenty, holds the fracture stable — fire traded for crystal, crystal traded for fire, and both traded for the blood-truce that keeps the orca at the shore. Theology rather than survival, for now. The seals hold. The veins still bleed their fire-cores. The aurora still refracts across the crystal spires. The orca still sing their pressure-songs at the Corpse Current, still pass the Dreaming Tooth from shaman to shaman, still try to resuscitate what the surface-dwellers have furnished into a civilization.
    
    But the body remembers what the name forgot, and the window in which these stories feel safe to tell is a window, not a door.
    
    ---
    
    ## V. THE SEALED AND THE UNSEALED
    
    Keeper Hollows camps at the Glow-Fissure's upper reaches. He was a cartographer once. His feathers have bleached to the color of old bone. His mining chart shows tunnels below the known depths — a fifth branch in patterns no living miner mapped — and the annotations are not in his handwriting, and the ink changes in the night, new routes appearing in wet strokes even when the page has been sealed beneath stone.
    
    He hears counting from below the ice-locks.
    
    Not knocking — not anymore. The sequence is thirteen, then silence, then twenty-one, and it is structured enough to imply a source. He tapped back once, and the counting answered. He will not say what the rhythm wants. He will not say why he stays. The deep-singers will tell you the god is trying to remember its own name. The fire-keepers will tell you the body is mapping its own interior. The Aurora liturgists do not speak of it.
    
    The Obelisk, cracked down its full length but unbroken, sits higher in its chamber than where it was last placed. Rising. The coral at its base, charred and lifeless since the great casting, has — according to the most recent Wake-Singer report — begun to show a single pale tendril of new growth.
    
    High-Beak Auditor Selka was found frozen in the eastern galleries after the great Calving split the Berg's face — found mid-reach toward a door that no longer existed. During the Long Dark, her translucent form emerged from the wall of the Hearth Plaza and watched the living for three hours without speaking before the veil drew her back into the ice-memory. She had not filed anything. Her claim remained open. The shapers became the aurora, and now Selka has become the aurora too — absorbed back into the god's body, still measuring, still counting, no longer able to intervene.
    
    Shadow-Broker Nyla climbed to the Lens-Cathedral in full aurora-light and confessed to thirty winters of custodianship that looked indistinguishable from corruption — the last in a line that began when the first shaper burrowed into the god's flesh. She vanished into the deep tunnels afterward. She was later confirmed dead, though the circumstances were never established. The door she guarded has no guardian now. The seal she maintained holds by habit rather than force.
    
    Grandmother Deep still swims. Her right eye — milk-white, pupilless — tracks things that are not there or are not yet. She dove to touch the god's body in the deep, and what she saw does not match any tradition. She carries scars in the old tongue, the language the dead god's followers abandoned three generations ago when the corpse stopped answering.
    
    The body remembers what the name forgot.
    
    The ice weeps near the Obelisk. The aurora will not touch it. The deep-singers cannot read it. It was here before the god was young, and it will be here when the last fire-core sputters and the last crystal spire goes dark and the frozen sea closes over the Berg like water over a wound.
    
    Before the shapers, the Obelisk waited.
    
    It waits still.
    
    ---
    
    *Compiled at Light Ingjeizhi under clear sky. Sixty-third session. Three traditions; one Berg; no resolution. The compiler sets down the text and seals the archive-ice and notes, for whatever scholar reads this when the window has closed: the counting has not stopped. The Obelisk has risen another inch. And the aurora, last night, pulsed in a color none of us could name — though Zheii Rukar', the youngest mystic, wept at it, and would not say why, and has not spoken since.*
