  annotation "note_1771591889779_0", anchor: "Set Down in the Archive-Ice at Light Ingjeizhi During the Sixty-Third Clear Sky of the Great Thaw", text: "The Great Thaw spans twenty-seven tracked intervals. Sixty-three of anything exceeds its duration by more than double. Either the compiler uses an internal calendrical system and provides no conversion — scholarly malpractice — or this was composed during the Faction Wars and mislabeled. I lean toward incompetence before conspiracy, but the distinction matters less when the result is identical: an undatable document wearing a date like a costume.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771591889779_1", anchor: "the blood-truce that keeps the orca at the shore", text: "No blood-truce existed during the Great Thaw. Deep-ice impressions from this period record orcas as distant shapes on patrol circuits — feared, mythologized, not counterparties to negotiated territorial agreements. A named blood-truce implies enforcement infrastructure that belongs to the late Faction Wars at earliest. I flagged an identical anachronism in 'Two Halves of the Vastness' and called it structurally honest embellishment. Here I am less generous. The compiler is projecting the present backward to make the Accord feel inevitable rather than contingent. That is not mythology. That is politics.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771591889779_2", anchor: "He hears counting from below the ice-locks", text: "Structured numerical sequence. Response when tapped. Annotations appearing overnight in unknown handwriting on a sealed map. The compiler frames this as theological evidence — the god's respiration, its self-cartography. Strip the doctrine away and what remains is contact with presences beneath the ice that predate penguin civilization. Patient. Structured. Responsive. The Nightshelf claims the watchers are real; the Aurora claims belief makes them so. Hollows tapped back and something answered. That is neither theology nor madness. That is correspondence, and it has been two-directional for longer than anyone on either side of the Accord wants to acknowledge.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771591889779_3", anchor: "the light is the inheritance", text: "Crown-light, heart-warmth, and the orca's pressure-deep grief. Three traditions, three claimed inheritances — mapping precisely onto the three sources of magic practiced on the Berg: aurora, fire, and pressure-depth. The compiler presents a theological dispute. Read structurally, it is a jurisdictional partition of magical practice dressed as creation myth.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771591889779_4", anchor: "the Aurora tradition does not mention the Glow-Fissure", text: "The compiler deserves credit for noting this absence and none for failing to interrogate it. The Aurora liturgy catalogs the god's transformation in exhaustive anatomical detail — brow-ridges, vertebrae, teeth, sinew, marrow — and omits the single feature all three traditions should address: the wound. Either the Glow-Fissure contradicts the voluntary-gift narrative so fundamentally that no liturgist could incorporate it without the theology collapsing, or the sixty-second verse was redacted. Both options are damning. Silence this systematic is not oversight. It is load-bearing doctrine.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771591889779_5", anchor: "the last in a line that began when the first shaper burrowed into the god's flesh", text: "The line was not unbroken and not exclusively Nightshelf. Nyla's predecessor was Aurora-born. I have corrected this in four entries now. The administrative ice is unambiguous. Mythologizing the Claws' custodial role as ancient Nightshelf inheritance is exactly the kind of romantic fiction this compiler should have caught.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771591889779_6", anchor: "All three traditions agree on one detail, and only one", text: "They also agree on the body. All three posit a primordial form that became the Berg — swimming, luminous, or warm. The compiler's claim of singular agreement is rhetorically effective and factually wrong. What makes the Obelisk remarkable is not that it is the sole convergence point but that it is the sole *inexplicable* one. Sloppy framing in a document that otherwise earns its ambitions. Infuriating.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
